  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">Les capacit&#xe9;s institutionnelles des organisations de travailleurs sont renforc&#xe9;es</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="FR">In Cameroon, workers&#x2019; organizations have historically faced fragmentation, limiting their representativeness and influence in social dialogue and policy processes. In this context, the International Labour Organization (ILO), in collaboration with its tripartite constituents, supports efforts to strengthen trade union unity, notably through initiatives such as the Cameroon Workers&#x2019; Forum (CAWOF), while enhancing the institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations to increase their impact in labour governance.

Significant progress has been achieved, particularly in advancing gender equality within trade unions. Several national trade union organizations have adopted a joint gender policy with an associated action plan, contributing to the institutionalization of gender mainstreaming in union governance and social dialogue. Women&#x2019;s participation in social dialogue processes has also increased through targeted training, coaching and the establishment of coordination mechanisms that promote their representation.

Building on these achievements, the programme aims to further strengthen the governance, representativeness and sustainability of workers&#x2019; organizations. It supports the implementation of gender- and youth-responsive reforms and the development of innovative strategies to reach and represent new groups of workers, including those in the informal economy. Particular attention is given to the formalization of workers by strengthening the capacity of trade unions to organize and represent informal workers and advocate for their access to rights, social protection and economic opportunities. The programme also promotes the engagement of workers&#x2019; organizations in emerging policy areas, including climate change, through just transition approaches. Overall, these interventions contribute to more inclusive, representative and influential trade unions that effectively participate in social dialogue and shape policies towards greater equity and formalization.</narrative>
  </description>
